{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01364 - MONDO_2313",
  "verbalization": "Ephedrine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Vernal Conjunctivitis through a series of indirect pathways, all of which involve Atopic Conjunctivitis. The drug is linked to Atopic Conjunctivitis via its indication for this condition, and Atopic Conjunctivitis is further connected to Vernal Conjunctivitis through the indications of Alcaftadine, Tromethamine, and Emedastine. These relationships suggest that Ephedrine may influence the immune response in a manner that contributes to the development of Vernal Conjunctivitis. The mechanisms underlying these connections are not fully elucidated, but they likely involve modulation of inflammatory pathways or immune cell activity, which are central to the disease process. The evidence is consistent across multiple pathways, reinforcing the possibility that Ephedrine could be a candidate for repurposing in the treatment of Vernal Conjunctivitis, provided further mechanistic validation is conducted.",
